A component imported from another package has its body inlined.

passNoInlineComponents walked `main` and the windows -- every root a *program*
declares, and none of the ones the pass itself elects. An imported component is
one: nothing inlines it, so the backend reads its declared body, and what that
body wrote was never inlined into it.

`sngl:ui/markup`'s `list` is the case that found it -- a vbox with an indent
and a bullet column, an ordinary library component with a body. It reached
codegen as a node under its own name and html wrote `<list>` and `<listItem>`,
elements no browser has heard of, with the layout they describe missing and
nothing said about it. The same tree written in `app.sngl` inlines and always
did, which is why the gap was invisible: it is the *import* that leaves the
body standing, not the tree.

`paragraph` beside it would not have shown this. It is pure, so passInlinePure
substitutes it on a walk that does reach an imported body; `list` provides a
`listDepth` context and so is impure, which is what routes it here.

deny * `<list\b` -- `markup.list` is a bodied library component and inlines away; nothing is emitted under its own name
deny * `<listItem\b` -- and neither is its item

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import doc "./doc"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="list") {
    doc.article
}
-- doc/article.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import markup "sngl:ui/markup"

component article ui.node {
    markup.list {
        markup.listItem {
            ui.text(value="one")
        }
    }
}
